<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rebellion Losses Bill</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rebellion Losses Bill was a law passed by the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1849 to compensate residents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada-east">Canada East</a> whose property had been damaged during the rebellions of 1837–1838. Tory opponents claimed it rewarded rebels, and when Governor General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> signed it, a mob in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> attacked him and burned the parliament building. Elgin's assent confirmed the principle of responsible government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill.jpg" alt="Rebellion Losses Bill" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Duncan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parlement_Canada_Montr%C3%A9al_int%C3%A9rieur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
